From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: the GPU sandbox lets drivers ask the scheduling
 priority range

NVIDIA's driver threads ask for the scheduling priority range with
sched_get_priority_max/min as they start, as they do in the video decoder
(0035). Since the GPU sandbox can read Gentoo's /usr/lib64 (0078), the
libraries that start them load in the GPU process too, and the GPU policy
didn't allow the two calls: SIGSYS at the GPU process's start, no GPU, and
Chrome, with no software compositing, quit before the login screen
(the NVIDIA test PC, build 23j, 2026-10-21). The renderer policy allows both; they
only return two fixed numbers.

diff --git a/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc b/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc
index eb74abbdcb6d2..72c512c98a1df 100644
--- a/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc
+++ b/sandbox/policy/linux/bpf_gpu_policy_linux.cc
@@ -50,6 +50,16 @@ ResultExpr GpuProcessPolicy::EvaluateSyscall(int sysno) const {
   switch (sysno) {
     case __NR_kcmp:
       return Error(ENOSYS);
+    // NickelOS: NVIDIA's driver threads ask for the scheduling priority
+    // range as they start, as they do in the video decoder (0035). Since
+    // the sandbox can read Gentoo's /usr/lib64 (0078), the libraries that
+    // start them load in the GPU process too, which died at its start with
+    // SIGSYS: no GPU, and Chrome, with no software compositing, quit before
+    // the login screen (the NVIDIA test PC, build 23j, 2026-10-21). The answer is two
+    // fixed numbers.
+    case __NR_sched_get_priority_max:
+    case __NR_sched_get_priority_min:
+      return Allow();
 #if !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
     case __NR_fallocate:
       return Allow();
